      "dc:source": "act.pdf, p.56",
      "values:originalText": "(2) A person is guilty of an offence if:\n\n(a) the person makes a threat that goods will be contaminated;\nand\n\n(b) the person does so with intent to cause:\n\n(i) public alarm or anxiety; or\n\n(ii) economic loss through public awareness of the\ncontamination, or possible contamination, of the goods;\nand\n\n(c) any of the following subparagraphs applies:\n\n(i) the loss is a loss to a constitutional corporation (other\nthan a foreign corporation within the meaning of\nparagraph 51(xx) of the Constitution);\n\n(ii) the loss is a loss to a constitutional corporation that is a\nforeign corporation within the meaning of paragraph\n51(xx) of the Constitution and the goods have been\nproduced, manufactured, assembled or otherwise\nprocessed in Australia;\n\n(iii) the goods belong to a constitutional corporation (other\nthan a foreign corporation within the meaning of\nparagraph 51(xx) of the Constitution);\n\n(iv) the goods belong to a constitutional corporation that is a\nforeign corporation within the meaning of paragraph\n51(xx) of the Constitution and the goods have been\nproduced, manufactured, assembled or otherwise\nprocessed in Australia;\n\n(v) the person is a constitutional corporation (other than a\nforeign corporation within the meaning of paragraph\n51(xx) of the Constitution);\n\n(vi) the person is a constitutional corporation that is a\nforeign corporation within the meaning of paragraph\n51(xx) of the Constitution and the goods have been\n\nContamination offence  Schedule 2\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Telecommunications Offences and Other\nMeasures) Act (No. 2) 2004                    51\nproduced, manufactured, assembled or otherwise\nprocessed in Australia;\n\n(vii) the loss takes the form of detriment to constitutional\ntrade and commerce;\n\n(viii) the goods are in the course of, or intended for,\nconstitutional trade and commerce;\n\n(ix) the person makes the threat in Australia using a postal\nor other like service or an electronic communication;\n\n(x) the person makes the threat outside Australia and the\ngoods have been produced, manufactured, assembled or\notherwise processed in Australia;\n\n(xi) the loss is a loss to the Commonwealth or a\nCommonwealth authority;\n\n(xii) the threat is made to the Commonwealth or a\nCommonwealth authority.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 10 years.",
      "skos:definition": "(2) A person is guilty of an offence if:\n\n(a) the person makes a threat that goods will be contaminated;\nand\n\n(b) the person does so with intent to cause:\n\n(i) public alarm or anxiety; or\n\n(ii) economic loss through public awareness of the\ncontamination, or possible contamination, of the goods;\nand\n\n(c) any of the following subparagraphs applies:\n\n(i) the loss is a loss to a constitutional corporation (other\nthan a foreign corporation within the meaning of\nparagraph 51(xx) of the Constitution);\n\n(ii) the loss is a loss to a constitutional corporation that is a\nforeign corporation within the meaning of paragraph\n51(xx) of the Constitution and the goods have been\nproduced, manufactured, assembled or otherwise\nprocessed in Australia;\n\n(iii) the goods belong to a constitutional corporation (other\nthan a foreign corporation within the meaning of\nparagraph 51(xx) of the Constitution);\n\n(iv) the goods belong to a constitutional corporation that is a\nforeign corporation within the meaning of paragraph\n51(xx) of the Constitution and the goods have been\nproduced, manufactured, assembled or otherwise\nprocessed in Australia;\n\n(v) the person is a constitutional corporation (other than a\nforeign corporation within the meaning of paragraph\n51(xx) of the Constitution);\n\n(vi) the person is a constitutional corporation that is a\nforeign corporation within the meaning of paragraph\n51(xx) of the Constitution and the goods have been\n\nContamination offence  Schedule 2\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Telecommunications Offences and Other\nMeasures) Act (No. 2) 2004                    51\nproduced, manufactured, assembled or otherwise\nprocessed in Australia;\n\n(vii) the loss takes the form of detriment to constitutional\ntrade and commerce;\n\n(viii) the goods are in the course of, or intended for,\nconstitutional trade and commerce;\n\n(ix) the person makes the threat in Australia using a postal\nor other like service or an electronic communication;\n\n(x) the person makes the threat outside Australia",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:act-sch-2-sec-50"
      },
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:act-sch-2-sec-50-ss-2-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:act-sch-2-sec-50-ss-2-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:act-sch-2-sec-50-ss-2-logic-alleninterval-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:act-sch-2-sec-50-ss-2-logic-deontic-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:act-sch-2-sec-50-ss-2-logic-deontic-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "e4b037a2b2c99e78eb1f4007e8aa3099371ad51c2317da8e3c989ffa3183eec7",
      "skos:note": "Making a threat of contamination with intent to cause public alarm or economic loss is an offence."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/act#sch-2-sec-50-ss-2",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "50(2) Crimes Legislation Amendment (Telecommunications Offences and",
      "values:kind": "subsection",
      "cof:pageNumber": 56,
      "values:originalText": "(2) A person is guilty of an offence if:\n\n(a) the person makes a threat that goods will be contaminated;\nand\n\n(b) the person does so with intent to cause:\n\n(i) public alarm or anxiety; or\n\n(ii) economic loss through public awareness of the\ncontamination, or possible contamination, of the goods;\nand\n\n(c) any of the following subparagraphs applies:\n\n(i) the loss is a loss to a constitutional corporation (other\nthan a foreign corporation within the meaning of\nparagraph 51(xx) of the Constitution);\n\n(ii) the loss is a loss to a constitutional corporation that is a\nforeign corporation within the meaning of paragraph\n51(xx) of the Constitution and the goods have been\nproduced, manufactured, assembled or otherwise\nprocessed in Australia;\n\n(iii) the goods belong to a constitutional corporation (other\nthan a foreign corporation within the meaning of\nparagraph 51(xx) of the Constitution);\n\n(iv) the goods belong to a constitutional corporation that is a\nforeign corporation within the meaning of paragraph\n51(xx) of the Constitution and the goods have been\nproduced, manufactured, assembled or otherwise\nprocessed in Australia;\n\n(v) the person is a constitutional corporation (other than a\nforeign corporation within the meaning of paragraph\n51(xx) of the Constitution);\n\n(vi) the person is a constitutional corporation that is a\nforeign corporation within the meaning of paragraph\n51(xx) of the Constitution and the goods have been\n\nContamination offence  Schedule 2\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Telecommunications Offences and Other\nMeasures) Act (No. 2) 2004                    51\nproduced, manufactured, assembled or otherwise\nprocessed in Australia;\n\n(vii) the loss takes the form of detriment to constitutional\ntrade and commerce;\n\n(viii) the goods are in the course of, or intended for,\nconstitutional trade and commerce;\n\n(ix) the person makes the threat in Australia using a postal\nor other like service or an electronic communication;\n\n(x) the person makes the threat outside Australia and the\ngoods have been produced, manufactured, assembled or\notherwise processed in Australia;\n\n(xi) the loss is a loss to the Commonwealth or a\nCommonwealth authority;\n\n(xii) the threat is made to the Commonwealth or a\nCommonwealth authority.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 10 years."
    },
